From e02b90058cdba09ea34ff08c1b5b903a0274f7b8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 21:35:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(textures): GRAIN-01 - three quarters of two tiles was a
 lighting ramp, not wood
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

WHAT WAS WRONG

Six photographic tiles shipped. Three of them read on a floor as something
other than wood, and the reason was not the one this repository had written
down.

Rendered at true floor scale with the board seams drawn — which is the test
that had never been run, because every contact sheet until now showed tiles at
equal PIXEL size rather than at equal INCHES, and so showed red oak repeating
4 times where a board shows it 2.5 — the set divided cleanly:

  red oak, white ash   a horizontal BAND crossing every board at a fixed
                       interval
  white oak            a blocky patchwork
  black walnut         a repeating arc motif, reading as woven basket
  hickory, hard maple   correct

THE BAND, WHICH WAS THE LARGEST DEFECT IN THE SET AND WAS NOT BEING MEASURED

flatten() divides the room's light out at a three-inch radius. That is right
for a window falling across a floor and blind to what is left: a slow
end-to-end drift down the strip, one end brighter than the other. A tile is
sampled as a torus, so the drift does not fade out at the end of the tile — it
steps back to the start, and the step is a band.

Measured as the standard deviation of the row means against the tile's own
contrast:

  red oak      11.2 of 15.5 grey levels
  white ash    12.4 of 16.3
  everything else  at or under 2.6

On two of the six tiles roughly three quarters of everything a person saw was
the lighting rather than the wood.

Nothing was measuring it. seam_step() compares the FIRST and LAST row, and a
ramp passes that check — the two ends can be made to meet by the wrap while
everything between them still slopes. Sixty-six guards and seventeen test
files, all green, all blind to it.

deband() levels the row-mean profile. ROWS ONLY: a row crosses the grain, so
its mean is lighting, while a COLUMN's mean is the grain's own cross-section
and levelling that deletes the wood and leaves pore noise. It runs on both
sides of the wrap, because the roll-blend perturbs the row means it has just
matched — white ash came out of the wrap with 1.5 grey levels restored from
the 12.7 removed, which is still a visible line at ash's contrast.

  red oak    10.3 → 0.1     white oak   1.8 → 0.2
  white ash  12.7 → 0.2     walnut      3.0 → 0.1
  hickory     2.4 → 0.2     maple       0.9 → 0.2

The four tiles that were not banded lose between 0.05 and 0.32 grey levels of
contrast for it.

THE CROPS, RE-CHOSEN BY MEASURING THE FINISHED TILE

The earlier search scored the RAW crop — before flatten() had divided the
light out — so it was largely ranking photographs by how evenly they were lit.
What ships is the tile, so the tile is what is scored now: low-frequency energy
at a one-inch physical radius, structure-tensor coherence, the seam, and a
penalty for repeating more than 1.6 times across a five-inch board.

  white oak    seam 0.147 → 0.055. At -18.1° of rotation the inscribed strip
               ran the whole height of the board and its ends picked up the
               bevel; that seam was nearly twice anything else in the set and
               is what the patchwork was. Best of 232 candidates.
  red oak      2.25in wide at 2.22 repeats per board, from 1.96in at 2.55. The
               band had been masking this: with it gone the same board yields a
               wider, calmer crop than the search could see before.
  black walnut seam 0.068 → 0.041.

The score was NOT followed blindly, and it should not be. On walnut and again
on red oak the top-scoring candidate was the worst of the set to look at: its
coherence came from broad smooth swirls, which the score rewards and the eye
reads as burl veneer. Both times the four best candidates were rendered at
floor scale and compared, and both times the one chosen scored slightly lower.
That is written in the crop notes beside each one.

WHITE ASH WAS LEFT ALONE, DELIBERATELY

245 candidates were scored inside its board and every one of them came out
2.1in wide — 2.4 repeats per board against the current crop's 1.08 — and
mid-brown, which is the wrong colour for a species the catalogue describes as
pale. The current crop is the right colour and barely repeats within a board.
Its remaining softness is in the photograph, not in the recipe: it is the
sharpest tile in the set per inch (111 px/in against hickory's 88).

THE GUARDS

Two added to the build script, both of which the recipe now satisfies with
room to spare, and neither of which narrows anything that was previously
allowed:

  - it refuses a set whose worst residual band is over half a grey level
    (currently 0.19)
  - grain.test.ts asserts the same from the manifest, and asserts that
    rowBandBefore is large somewhere in the set — a recipe that silently
    stopped levelling would otherwise pass by having nothing to level

No existing guard was widened or relaxed. The red oak crop was chosen partly
so that the existing `inchesAcross > 2` assertion did not have to move.

VERIFIED

  65 of 66 guards pass. verify:css fails identically on a clean checkout in
  this sandbox — postcss is unresolvable with no node_modules — and is
  unrelated to this change.
  All 26 grain.test.ts assertions checked by hand against the built set, since
  vitest cannot install here.
  scripts/render-api-test.mjs: ALL PASSED, including that all six API copies
  measure what the catalogue says.
  Rendered through renderFloorPlate() at 1100x820 for four species: no
  banding, no patchwork, no quilting.

